WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.061
Picture the company behind a coffee-shop payments app, checking its numbers at the month's end.

2
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:11.376
Ivy's spending account started the month at 50.00.

3
00:00:11.376 --> 00:00:17.584
On the first morning, in one second, two charges came in: 10.00 for a coffee, and a 10.00 monthly fee.

4
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.438
It should show 30.00.

5
00:00:19.438 --> 00:00:20.608
It shows 40.00.

6
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.756
Both charges are in the list of payments.

7
00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:25.637
Both programs said success.

8
00:00:25.637 --> 00:00:26.891
No error anywhere.

9
00:00:26.891 --> 00:00:28.841
So where did ten dollars go?

10
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.302
Let's put it in the database.

11
00:00:31.302 --> 00:00:38.137
The company keeps its data in PostgreSQL, a free database program, in tables made of rows.

12
00:00:38.137 --> 00:00:42.693
Ivy's account is row 109, and it keeps the balance in cents.

13
00:00:42.693 --> 00:00:44.060
So 50.00 is 5,000.

14
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:50.799
Each charge runs in a transaction, a group of statements the database applies together or not at all.

15
00:00:50.799 --> 00:00:58.713
And each does three things we've all written: read the balance, subtract in its own Kotlin code, and write the result back.

16
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:05.111
Run them one after the other, and the balance goes 5,000, 4,000, 3,000.

17
00:01:05.111 --> 00:01:06.423
So far so good.

18
00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:11.586
Now, you might be thinking: the database won't let two writes on one row clash.

19
00:01:11.586 --> 00:01:13.080
And you'd be half right.

20
00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:23.004
When a transaction updates a row, PostgreSQL puts a row lock on it, a mark that makes any other writer of that row wait until this transaction commits.

21
00:01:23.267 --> 00:01:25.172
So let the two overlap.

22
00:01:25.172 --> 00:01:28.817
The payment reads 5,000 and works out 4,000.

23
00:01:28.817 --> 00:01:32.545
The fee reads 5,000 too, and works out 4,000.

24
00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:35.766
The payment writes 4,000 and takes the lock.

25
00:01:35.766 --> 00:01:38.084
The fee tries to write, and waits.

26
00:01:38.084 --> 00:01:39.720
Good, it's taking turns.

27
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.850
The payment commits, which makes its change permanent, and lets go of the lock.

28
00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:46.568
What does the fee write now?

29
00:01:46.568 --> 00:01:47.551
Pause and guess.

30
00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:51.159
4,000.

31
00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:54.139
The number it worked out before it waited.

32
00:01:54.139 --> 00:01:57.687
Two charges ran, and the balance went down by one.

33
00:01:57.967 --> 00:01:58.508
Well...

34
00:01:58.508 --> 00:02:00.130
the lock did its job.

35
00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.680
It just wasn't the job we needed.

36
00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:05.345
Let's leave computers for a minute.

37
00:02:05.345 --> 00:02:11.009
Two flatmates share a grocery card, and its balance is on the whiteboard on the fridge: 50.

38
00:02:11.267 --> 00:02:13.394
In the morning, both glance at it.

39
00:02:13.394 --> 00:02:18.149
Each spends 10, works out 40 in their head, and comes home to write it down.

40
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.039
There's only one marker.

41
00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:24.408
So the second flatmate waits politely while the first writes 40.

42
00:02:24.408 --> 00:02:27.139
Then she takes the marker, and writes...

43
00:02:27.139 --> 00:02:27.344
40.

44
00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:32.305
Waiting her turn didn't help, because she brought an answer from the morning.

45
00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:33.430
Map it back.

46
00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:35.301
The whiteboard is row 109.

47
00:02:35.301 --> 00:02:37.244
The marker is the row lock.

48
00:02:37.244 --> 00:02:42.065
The glance is the read, and the sum in her head is the Kotlin code.

49
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.358
The lock put the two writes in order.

50
00:02:44.358 --> 00:02:47.305
It couldn't fix a number worked out from an old read.

51
00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:49.836
This has a name: a lost update.

52
00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:57.772
Two transactions read the same value, each writes a result worked out from it, and the second write erases the first.

53
00:02:57.772 --> 00:03:00.553
Nothing failed, so no log line tells you.

54
00:03:00.767 --> 00:03:07.894
The shape behind it is called a read-modify-write cycle: read a value, change it in the program, write it back.

55
00:03:07.894 --> 00:03:18.231
It hides in a counter of likes, in a program that loads a whole document and saves it back, and in a worker that reads which job is next and then marks it taken.

56
00:03:18.433 --> 00:03:20.347
So how do the flatmates fix it?

57
00:03:20.347 --> 00:03:21.952
Don't bring a number home.

58
00:03:21.952 --> 00:03:24.052
Bring an instruction: take 10 off.

59
00:03:24.052 --> 00:03:28.188
Whoever holds the marker reads the board right then, and subtracts.

60
00:03:28.433 --> 00:03:33.490
In SQL, that's one statement: set the balance to the balance minus 1,000.

61
00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:37.855
The read and the write happen in one step, inside the database.

62
00:03:38.133 --> 00:03:38.852
Same race.

63
00:03:38.852 --> 00:03:41.439
The payment's update takes the lock.

64
00:03:41.439 --> 00:03:43.092
The fee's update waits.

65
00:03:43.092 --> 00:03:44.529
The payment commits.

66
00:03:44.529 --> 00:03:46.901
Now what does the balance end at?

67
00:03:46.901 --> 00:03:48.051
Pause and guess.

68
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:51.668
3,000.

69
00:03:51.668 --> 00:03:59.555
When the lock went, PostgreSQL read row 109 again, as the payment left it, and subtracted from 4,000.

70
00:03:59.555 --> 00:04:03.615
Both charges are there, and nobody had to try again.

71
00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:09.975
Sometimes the new value needs logic SQL can't write, like a fraud score from another program.

72
00:04:09.975 --> 00:04:11.935
Then there are two more fixes.

73
00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:16.897
Lock when you read: SELECT FOR UPDATE takes the marker before you look.

74
00:04:16.897 --> 00:04:20.469
The fee's read waits for the payment, then sees 4,000.

75
00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:25.370
Or compare and set: write 4,000 only if the balance is still 5,000.

76
00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:32.340
If someone got there first, the update changes no rows, and the program reads again and tries again.

77
00:04:32.533 --> 00:04:36.610
A rule of thumb: lock rows that many transactions want at once.

78
00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.651
Compare and set on rows that are rarely shared.

79
00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:41.924
So, where did the ten dollars go?

80
00:04:41.924 --> 00:04:45.236
The fee wrote a number it worked out before it waited.

81
00:04:45.236 --> 00:04:47.015
The lock puts writes in line.

82
00:04:47.015 --> 00:04:48.609
It doesn't redo your sums.

83
00:04:48.867 --> 00:04:52.607
Let the database do the arithmetic, or make it check yours.

84
00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:54.419
One quick check.

85
00:04:54.419 --> 00:04:55.942
A post has 41 likes.

86
00:04:55.942 --> 00:05:02.645
Two people tap like at once, and each program reads 41, adds one in code, and writes 42.

87
00:05:02.645 --> 00:05:04.473
What does the post show?

88
00:05:04.473 --> 00:05:05.692
Pause and think.

89
00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:09.170
42.

90
00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:10.698
One like is lost.

91
00:05:10.698 --> 00:05:14.744
Set likes to likes plus one, and it shows 43.
